# Manuscript text. See build.py for the block types.
from experiments_text import EXPERIMENTS

INTRO = [
('h1', '1. Introduction'),

('p', 'Precise Point Positioning (PPP) uses the code and carrier-phase observations of a single receiver together with precise satellite orbits and clocks, and reaches centimetre-level accuracy without a nearby reference station. Two problems limit its use: the float solution needs tens of minutes to converge, and the undifferenced phase ambiguities are not integers because receiver and satellite hardware delays are absorbed into them. PPP with integer ambiguity resolution (PPP-AR) removes the second problem and shortens the first, provided that three conditions are met. The integer estimator must be sound, the decision to accept the integers must keep the rate of wrong fixes low, and the satellite bias products must come from the same orbit and clock solution that the user applies. Most studies have improved one of these conditions at a time. In this study, we examine what happens when they are treated together in one processing system.'),

('p', 'The estimation part is well developed. De Jonge et al. (1996) described the float, integer and fixed steps of the LAMBDA method and showed that most of its computing time is spent on decorrelation. Tiberius and de Jonge (1995) showed with real data that LAMBDA can find the correct integers from a single epoch on short baselines. Jung (2000) defined the Cascade Integer Resolution (CIR) for triple-frequency signals and linked each cascade step to an integrity risk. Teunissen et al. (2002) proved that TCAR and CIR are geometry-free bootstrapping estimators, whereas LAMBDA gives the integer least-squares (ILS) solution with the highest success rate. Chen (2011) reduced the size of the integer search by combining a local minima search with LAMBDA, and Liu (2003) compared eight RTK strategies in one Kalman filter. In all of these studies, however, the integer solution is accepted either with critical values taken from experience (Tiberius and de Jonge, 1995; Chen, 2011), with a fixed ratio threshold (Liu, 2003), or without a test (de Jonge et al., 1996; Teunissen et al., 2002). Chen (2011) states that the validation of the whole ambiguity vector is still an open problem.'),

('p', 'The acceptance decision has been studied through the success rate and partial ambiguity resolution (PAR). Verhagen et al. (2013) showed that the bootstrapped success rate of the decorrelated ambiguities is a tight lower bound of the ILS success rate. Verhagen et al. (2011) proposed two ways to choose the subset to fix: the largest subset that reaches a required success rate, and the removal of satellites until the fixed failure-rate ratio test (FFRT) accepts the solution. Psychas et al. (2020) showed in a global formal analysis that adding Galileo to GPS shortens the 90th-percentile time to first fix from 25.5 to 8.5 min. Ge et al. (2005) raised the fixing rate of a global network from 74% to more than 97% by fixing the ambiguities in the order of their Dong–Bock probability. Duong (2020) compared PAR with the iFlex and best integer equivariant estimators for multi-frequency PPP-AR. These success rates are only valid if the float ambiguities are unbiased. Verhagen et al. (2011, 2013) left the case of biased data open, the results of Psychas et al. (2020) are formal, and the ordering of Ge et al. (2005) does not use the correlations between ambiguities. With real products the float ambiguities are often biased, so a rule that controls wrong fixes in this situation is still needed.'),

('p', 'Satellite bias products have moved from calibration studies to operational services. Banville et al. (2008) showed how receiver and satellite code and phase biases are absorbed into the clocks and ambiguities, and Delporte et al. (2008) removed the frequency drift of carrier-phase time transfer by fixing single-difference ambiguities. Ge et al. (2008) made single-receiver ambiguity resolution possible with between-satellite uncalibrated phase delays (UPDs). Collins et al. (2010) proposed the decoupled clock model (DCM), and integer phase clocks, as used in the CNES products, became a third approach (Delporte et al., 2008; Laurichesse and Blot, 2016). Shi and Gao (2014) proved that these three user models give the same position once the ambiguities are fixed correctly, but they did not compare them with data. On the network side, Li and Zhang (2012) estimated the fractional offsets of receivers and satellites in one adjustment. Geng et al. (2012) showed that fractional cycle biases (FCBs) from an ambiguity-fixed network are more accurate and that their error decreases as the network becomes denser, and Li et al. (2016) turned this approach into an open FCB service. Geng et al. (2019a), Ruan and Wei (2019) and Dai et al. (2019) improved phase clock, between-satellite network and real-time clock products. Three problems remain in this line of work. First, the user models have only been compared analytically (Shi and Gao, 2014), so their behaviour before the ambiguities are fixed is unknown. Second, network FCBs are usually judged by their own residuals or fixing rates rather than at stations left out of the estimation (Li and Zhang, 2012; Geng et al., 2012; Li et al., 2016). Third, the user cannot check whether the biases match the orbits and clocks being used: Ge et al. (2008) noted that narrow-lane UPDs absorb the modelling errors of the software that produced them, and Geng et al. (2019a) assume that clocks and biases always come from the same source.'),

('p', 'More satellites and frequencies also shorten convergence. Bahadur (2017) showed that four constellations shorten float-PPP convergence by about one third. Öğütcü et al. (2023) found that BDS-3 improves kinematic availability but degrades static PPP-AR when all systems are weighted equally. Laurichesse and Blot (2016) reached 20 cm horizontal accuracy in two minutes with triple-frequency phase-only wide lanes and per-observable biases, and Gayatri et al. (2015) discussed the same type of bias. Both Bahadur (2017) and Öğütcü et al. (2023), however, use fixed weights between the systems, and the results of these studies depend on the products of a single provider. On the estimation side, Guo and Zhang (2014) combined the IGG-III robust function with one adaptive factor for all states and proposed separate factors for different groups as future work. Geng et al. (n.d.) traced wrong hourly fixes to float solutions biased by multipath and eclipsing satellites, Shi and Gao (2012) showed that ambiguity resolution improves tropospheric delay estimates, and Gu et al. (2017) applied ambiguity resolution to GRACE baselines. Of the open-source programs, PRIDE PPP-AR (Geng et al., 2019b) is a GPS-only batch solver with fixed acceptance rules, and PPP-ARISEN (Zhang et al., 2023) uses the variance of the wet delay (CSI-ZWDV) to decide when to start the integer search; this indicator was checked against position accuracy, not against the probability of a correct fix.'),

('p', 'This study presents an undifferenced GPS+Galileo PPP/PPP-AR software and evaluates it with real data. The software makes three main changes to a conventional PPP-AR processing chain.'),

('p', '**(1) One filter for all user models.** Float PPP and the DCM, between-satellite single-difference (SDBS) and integer phase clock (IPC) user models run in the same extended Kalman filter (EKF), with the same error models and update. The three models of Shi and Gao (2014) can therefore be compared on identical data. The filter also gives each observation type (code, phase and Melbourne–Wübbena of each system) its own variance factor, which is the grouped adaptation proposed by Guo and Zhang (2014) and replaces the fixed system weights of Bahadur (2017) and Öğütcü et al. (2023). The physical model includes satellite antenna variations, the satellite attitude of the analysis centre, ocean and pole tides and the Shapiro delay, which address the error sources named by Geng et al. (n.d.) and Öğütcü et al. (2023).'),

('p', '**(2) A decision that limits wrong fixes.** The two subset strategies of Verhagen et al. (2011) are combined: the candidates are ordered by their Dong–Bock probability (Ge et al., 2005), the subset is chosen by the bootstrapped success rate (Verhagen et al., 2013), the integers are accepted with the FFRT, and a satellite is removed when the test fails. This replaces fixed ratio thresholds (Li et al., 2016; Geng et al., 2019a; Gu et al., 2017) and adds the validation that is missing in Collins et al. (2010). Two machine-learning components target biased float solutions, the case left open by Verhagen et al. (2011, 2013): an acceptance test trained on simulations with injected biases, and a fixability model trained, without manual labels, on arcs that converge later in the same session. The second model also decides when the integer search starts, which is the role of the CSI-ZWDV indicator of Zhang et al. (2023).'),

('p', '**(3) Checked products.** FCBs are estimated from a station network with a batch adjustment (Li and Zhang, 2012), a Kalman filter and a Kalman filter with learned noise, and every estimate is tested at stations left out of the estimation, which Li and Zhang (2012), Geng et al. (2012) and Li et al. (2016) did not do. Before any integer is fixed, the software reads the analysis centre of each product from its file header and checks with a narrow-lane test whether the float ambiguities cluster at integers, as they do in consistent systems (Ge et al., 2008; Dai et al., 2019). The product consistency assumed by Ge et al. (2008) and Geng et al. (2019a) thus becomes a test.'),

('p', 'The software is evaluated with 24 h of GPS+Galileo data from the IGS station MADR. In the first experiment, PPP and the three PPP-AR models are compared using the products of four analysis centres, each with its own orbits and clocks. In the second experiment, the FCBs are estimated from a network of IGS stations around MADR and used in the same comparison. The contribution of each new component is then measured step by step. Triple-frequency combinations (Jung, 2000; Laurichesse and Blot, 2016; Gayatri et al., 2015; Duong, 2020), BeiDou and GLONASS, relative positioning (Liu, 2003; Chen, 2011; Gu et al., 2017), time transfer (Delporte et al., 2008), hardware calibration of receiver biases (Banville et al., 2008) and network clock estimation (Ruan and Wei, 2019; Dai et al., 2019) are not covered. Section 2 describes the methods, Section 3 the experiments and their results, and Section 4 summarises the conclusions.'),
]

METHODS = [
('h1', '2. Materials and Methods'),

('p', 'This section gives the observation model (Section 2.1), the error models (2.2), the common Kalman filter (2.3), the three PPP-AR user models with the product corrections (2.4), the integer decision (2.5), the machine-learning components (2.6), the network FCB estimation (2.7) and the narrow-lane product test (2.8). GPS uses the L1/L2 and Galileo the E1/E5a frequencies. All constants are system specific.'),

('h2', '2.1. Observation model'),

('p', 'For receiver $r$, satellite $s$ and frequency $i$, the code and carrier-phase observations in metres are'),
('eq', 'P_{r,i}^{s} = %rho_r^s + c(dt_r - dt^s) + T_r^s + I_{r,i}^s + d_{r,i} - d_i^s + %varepsilon_{P}'),
('eq', 'L_{r,i}^{s} = %rho_r^s + c(dt_r - dt^s) + T_r^s - I_{r,i}^s + %lambda_i (N_{r,i}^s + b_{r,i} - b_i^s + %phi_r^s) + %varepsilon_{L}'),
('p', 'where $%rho$ is the geometric range, $dt_r$ and $dt^s$ the receiver and satellite clock offsets, $T$ the tropospheric delay, $I_i$ the first-order ionospheric delay, $d$ and $b$ the receiver and satellite code and phase biases, $N_i$ the integer ambiguity, $%phi$ the phase wind-up in cycles and $%lambda_i = c/f_i$ the wavelength. The ionosphere-free (IF) and Melbourne–Wübbena (MW) combinations are'),
('eq', 'P_{"IF"} = {f_1^2 P_1 - f_2^2 P_2} over {f_1^2 - f_2^2}, ~~~~ L_{"IF"} = {f_1^2 L_1 - f_2^2 L_2} over {f_1^2 - f_2^2}'),
('eq', '"MW" = {f_1 L_1 - f_2 L_2} over {f_1 - f_2} - {f_1 P_1 + f_2 P_2} over {f_1 + f_2} = %lambda_W (N_W + b_{r,W} - b_W^s)'),
('p', 'with the wide-lane ambiguity $N_W = N_1 - N_2$ and wavelength $%lambda_W = c/(f_1 - f_2)$. All ambiguity-resolution models in this study write the IF ambiguity with $N_1$ and $N_W$:'),
('eq', 'B_{"IF"} = %lambda_N N_1 + %beta N_W, ~~~~ %lambda_N = c over {f_1 + f_2}, ~~~~ %beta = {c f_2} over {f_1^2 - f_2^2}'),
('p', 'For GPS $%lambda_N approx 10.7$ cm, $%beta approx 37.7$ cm and $%lambda_W approx 86.2$ cm. The ambiguities are therefore resolved in two steps, the wide lane from the MW combination and then $N_1$ from the IF phase. The raw zenith standard deviations $%sigma_P$ and $%sigma_L$ are scaled with the elevation $e$,'),
('eq', '%sigma_{P,i}(e) = %sigma_P over {"max"(0.1, sin e)}, ~~~~ %sigma_{L,i}(e) = %sigma_L over {"max"(0.1, sin e)}'),
('p', 'and propagated, including their correlations, to the IF code, IF phase and MW rows of each satellite with the linear transformation $bold T$ of the raw observations $(P_1, P_2, L_1, L_2)$:'),
('eq', 'bold R^s = bold T " diag"(%sigma_{P,1}^2, %sigma_{P,2}^2, %sigma_{L,1}^2, %sigma_{L,2}^2) bold T^T'),

('h2', '2.2. Error models'),

('p', 'Satellite positions are interpolated with a degree-nine Lagrange polynomial at the emission time and corrected for the Earth rotation during the signal travel time. The travel time is computed from the geometric distance to the approximate receiver position and not as $P/c$, because the pseudorange contains the receiver clock offset; at MADR this offset (−0.18 ms) would rotate the satellites by about 0.35 m and bias a 24 h float solution by 7.5 cm to the east. Satellite clocks are taken from 30 s clock files. The periodic relativistic clock correction and the Shapiro delay are'),
('eq', '%delta_{"rel"} = -{2 bold r^s cdot bold v^s} over c, ~~~~ %delta_{"Shapiro"} = {2 GM} over c^2 ln {r^s + r_r + %rho_r^s} over {r^s + r_r - %rho_r^s}'),
('p', 'where $GM$ is the geocentric gravitational constant. The Shapiro delay is about 1.3 cm at zenith and 1.9 cm near the horizon. The tropospheric delay is'),
('eq', 'T_r^s = m_h(e) " ZHD" + m_w(e) ( " ZWD"_0 + %DELTA " ZWD")'),
('p', 'with the Saastamoinen (1972) hydrostatic delay, the Niell (1996) mapping functions $m_h$ and $m_w$, an a priori wet delay $" ZWD"_0$ and an estimated residual $%DELTA " ZWD"$. A station displacement $%DELTA bold r$ shortens the observed range by $bold u cdot %DELTA bold r$, where $bold u$ is the unit vector from the receiver to the satellite. The solid Earth tide follows the IERS Conventions (Petit and Luzum, 2010, Eq. 7.5):'),
('eq', '%DELTA bold r_{"solid"} = sum from{j=2} to{3} {GM_j R_E^4} over {GM_E R_j^3} left( h_2 {hat bold r} left( 3 over 2 ({hat bold R}_j cdot {hat bold r})^2 - 1 over 2 right) + 3 l_2 ({hat bold R}_j cdot {hat bold r}) left( {hat bold R}_j - ({hat bold R}_j cdot {hat bold r}) {hat bold r} right) right)'),
('p', 'with the Moon and the Sun ($j = 2, 3$), $h_2 = 0.6078$ and $l_2 = 0.0847$. Ocean tide loading is computed from the eleven tidal constituents of a BLQ file,'),
('eq', '%DELTA c(t) = sum from{k=1} to{11} A_{c,k} cos left( %chi_k(t) - %PHI_{c,k} right), ~~~~ c in lbrace "up", "west", "south" rbrace'),
('p', 'where $A$ and $%PHI$ are the amplitudes and phase lags of the station and $%chi_k$ the astronomical arguments. The pole tide uses the polar motion $(x_p, y_p)$ of an ERP file relative to the IERS mean pole, $m_1 = x_p - overline x_p$ and $m_2 = -(y_p - overline y_p)$ in arcseconds, for colatitude $%theta$ and longitude $%lambda$, in millimetres:'),
('eq', 'S_r = -33 sin 2%theta (m_1 cos %lambda + m_2 sin %lambda), ~~~~ S_{%theta} = -9 cos 2%theta (m_1 cos %lambda + m_2 sin %lambda)'),
('eq', 'S_{%lambda} = 9 cos %theta (m_1 sin %lambda - m_2 cos %lambda)'),
('p', 'The satellite body axes $(bold e_x, bold e_y, bold e_z)$ follow the nominal yaw or, when the analysis centre provides an ORBEX attitude file, the rows of the rotation matrix of the quaternion $q = (q_0, q_1, q_2, q_3)$:'),
('eq', 'bold r_{"body"} = bold R(q) bold r_{"ECEF"}, ~~~~ bold R(q) = left( matrix{ 1 - 2(q_2^2 + q_3^2) # 2(q_1 q_2 + q_0 q_3) # 2(q_1 q_3 - q_0 q_2) ## 2(q_1 q_2 - q_0 q_3) # 1 - 2(q_1^2 + q_3^2) # 2(q_2 q_3 + q_0 q_1) ## 2(q_1 q_3 + q_0 q_2) # 2(q_2 q_3 - q_0 q_1) # 1 - 2(q_1^2 + q_2^2) } right)'),
('p', 'During eclipses and noon turns the nominal yaw can be wrong by up to 180°, so the measured attitude changes both the satellite antenna offset and the wind-up. The satellite antenna offset is applied along these axes, the satellite phase-centre variation is interpolated at the nadir angle, and the receiver offset and variation at the zenith angle. The phase wind-up is computed after Wu et al. (1993):'),
('eq', '%phi = func sign left( bold k cdot (bold D^s times bold D_r) right) {arccos left( {bold D^s cdot bold D_r} over { abs{bold D^s} abs{bold D_r} } right)} over {2 %pi}'),
('eq', 'bold D^s = bold e_x - bold k (bold k cdot bold e_x) - bold k times bold e_y, ~~~~ bold D_r = bold x_r - bold k (bold k cdot bold x_r) + bold k times bold y_r'),
('p', 'where $bold k$ is the unit vector from the satellite to the receiver and $(bold x_r, bold y_r)$ are the north and west axes of the receiver. The wind-up is unwrapped along each arc and removed from the IF phase as $%lambda_N %phi$.'),

('h2', '2.3. Common extended Kalman filter'),

('p', 'Float PPP and all PPP-AR models use one state vector and one epoch loop:'),
('eq', 'bold x = left( bold r_r^T, c dt_{r}^{"G"}, dotslow , " ZWD", bold a_1^T, dotslow , bold a_{68}^T, c dt_{r}^{"E"}, dotslow right)^T'),
('p', 'where $bold r_r$ is the receiver position, $c dt_r^{G}$ and $c dt_r^{E}$ are the receiver clock parameters of GPS and Galileo (none, two or three per system, depending on the user model), and $bold a_s$ is the ambiguity block of each of the 68 satellite slots (one IF ambiguity in float PPP, the pair $(N_1, N_W)$ in PPP-AR). The time update is'),
('eq', '{hat bold x}_k^{"−"} = {hat bold x}_{k-1}^{"+"}, ~~~~ bold P_k^{"−"} = bold P_{k-1}^{"+"} + bold Q_k'),
('eq', 'bold Q_{rr} = q_p^2 %DELTA t bold I_3 " (kinematic)", ~~~~ Q_{"ZWD"} = left( q_T over 1000 right)^2 {%DELTA t} over 3600'),
('p', 'The receiver clocks are reset to white noise ($10^{10}$ m²) at every epoch, and the ambiguities are constant within an arc. The measurement update is an iterated (Gauss–Newton) EKF with the Joseph form of the covariance:'),
('eq', 'bold K = bold P^{"−"} bold H^T (bold H bold P^{"−"} bold H^T + bold R)^{-1}, ~~~~ {hat bold x}^{(j+1)} = {hat bold x}^{"−"} + bold K left( bold y - h( {hat bold x}^{(j)} ) - bold H ( {hat bold x}^{"−"} - {hat bold x}^{(j)} ) right)'),
('eq', 'bold P^{"+"} = (bold I - bold K bold H) bold P^{"−"} (bold I - bold K bold H)^T + bold K bold R bold K^T'),
('p', 'Each user model supplies only its measurement function: the observations, their predicted values, the design matrix $bold H$, the covariance $bold R$ and the satellite of each row. Outliers are handled with the IGG-III scheme (Yang et al., 2002). Post-fit residuals are divided by their standard deviation from $bold R - bold H bold P^{"+"} bold H^T$, and the largest standardised residual $v_s$ of a satellite inflates all rows of that satellite by $1/w(v_s)$:'),
('eq', 'w(v) = left lbrace stack{ 1 # {k_0 over abs v} left( {k_1 - abs v} over {k_1 - k_0} right)^2 # 10^{-8} } right none ~~~~ stack{ abs v <= k_0 # k_0 < abs v <= k_1 # abs v > k_1 }'),
('p', 'with $k_0 = 2.5$ and $k_1 = 6.0$. Inflating all rows of a satellite keeps the update consistent when these rows are correlated, as in the between-satellite model.'),
('p', '**Variance factors per observation group.** In the adaptive filter, each observation group $g$ (code, phase and MW of each system) has its own variance factor $%alpha_g$, which multiplies its rows of $bold R$. The receiver clock enters all rows of a group equally, so the predicted innovations $bold v$ are centred over the $m_g$ rows of the group with $bold C = bold I - bold 1 bold 1^T / m_g$, which removes the clock. Rows of new ambiguities are excluded by using only rows whose predicted variance without the clock is smaller than their observation variance. The factor is a smoothed moment estimate,'),
('eq', '%alpha_g = { sum from{k} (1 - %lambda)^{n-k} sum from{i in g} left( {(bold C bold v_k)}_i^2 - {(bold C bold H_k bold P_k^{"−"} bold H_k^T bold C^T)}_{ii} right) } over { sum from{k} (1 - %lambda)^{n-k} sum from{i in g} {(bold C bold R_{0,k} bold C^T)}_{ii} }'),
('p', 'with $%lambda = 0.05$, $%alpha_g in left[ 0.2, 25 right]$ and $bold R_0$ the covariance before scaling. Predicted innovations are used because post-fit residuals depend on the factor itself and drive it towards its lower limit. The position covariance is inflated (fading; Yang et al., 2001) only for kinematic receivers and only when the normalised innovation squared exceeds its $%chi^2$ quantile,'),
('eq', 'T = {bold v^T bold S^{-1} bold v} over m > {%chi_{0.999}^2(m)} over m drarrow bold P_{rr}^{"−"} leftarrow {T m} over {%chi_{0.999}^2(m)} bold P_{rr}^{"−"}'),
('p', 'because a static receiver has no dynamic-model error to absorb.'),

('h2', '2.4. PPP-AR user models and product corrections'),

('p', 'In PPP-AR each satellite contributes an IF code, an IF phase and an MW row, to which the product corrections $c_P^s$, $c_L^s$ and $c_W^s$ are added:'),
('eq', 'y_P^s = P_{"IF"}^s + c_P^s, ~~~~ y_L^s = L_{"IF"}^s + c_L^s, ~~~~ y_W^s = "MW"^s + c_W^s'),
('eq', 'y_P^s = %rho_r^s + %delta^s + m_w %DELTA " ZWD" + c dt_{r,P}'),
('eq', 'y_L^s = %rho_r^s + %delta^s + m_w %DELTA " ZWD" + %lambda_N N_1^s + %beta N_W^s + c dt_{r,L}'),
('eq', 'y_W^s = %lambda_W N_W^s + c dt_{r,W}'),
('p', 'where $%delta^s$ contains the modelled delays of Section 2.2. For observable-specific signal biases (OSB) of the codes $C_i$ and phases $L_i$, the corrections, which include the satellite clock, are'),
('eq', 'c_P^s = c dt^s - {f_1^2 C_1^s - f_2^2 C_2^s} over {f_1^2 - f_2^2}, ~~~~ c_L^s = c dt^s - {f_1^2 L_1^s - f_2^2 L_2^s} over {f_1^2 - f_2^2}, ~~~~ c_W^s = -left( {f_1 L_1^s - f_2 L_2^s} over {f_1 - f_2} - {f_1 C_1^s + f_2 C_2^s} over {f_1 + f_2} right)'),
('p', 'For FCB products $b_1^s$, $b_2^s$ (cycles) they are $c_P^s = c dt^s$, $c_L^s = c dt^s - (%gamma_1 %lambda_1 b_1^s + %gamma_2 %lambda_2 b_2^s)$ and $c_W^s = -%lambda_W (b_1^s - b_2^s)$, with $%gamma_1 = f_1^2/(f_1^2 - f_2^2)$ and $%gamma_2 = -f_2^2/(f_1^2 - f_2^2)$. A satellite without an FCB stays in the float solution but is neither an AR candidate nor a reference satellite, and its arc restarts when it gains or loses an FCB, because the correction then jumps by a fraction of a cycle.'),
('p', 'The user models differ in how they remove the remaining receiver biases (Shi and Gao, 2014). **Decoupled clock model (DCM; Collins et al., 2010).** Three receiver clocks per system, $c dt_{r,P}$, $c dt_{r,L}$ and $c dt_{r,W}$, are estimated as white noise. The rank defect between the phase clock and the ambiguities is removed by setting the ambiguities of a reference satellite $q$ to zero, $N_1^q = N_W^q = 0$. **Between-satellite single differences (SDBS; Ge et al., 2008).** The receiver clocks are removed by differencing each satellite with the reference satellite of its own system, and the covariance is propagated:'),
('eq', 'bold y_{"SD"} = (bold D otimes bold I_3) bold y, ~~~~ bold R_{"SD"} = (bold D otimes bold I_3) bold R (bold D otimes bold I_3)^T'),
('eq', 'D_{jk} = left lbrace stack{ 1 # -1 # 0 } right none ~~~~ stack{ k = j # k = q(j) # "otherwise" }'),
('p', '**Integer phase clock (IPC; Laurichesse and Blot, 2016).** The MW rows are not used in the filter. The wide lane of each satellite is rounded from its arc mean $overline m^s$ (cycles) after subtracting a receiver wide-lane bias. This bias is a circular mean, so fractions close to ±0.5 do not cancel each other:'),
('eq', 'b_{r,W} = 1 over {2 %pi} "arg" left( sum from{s} e^{2 %pi i overline m^s} right), ~~~~ N_W^s = "round" left( overline m^s - b_{r,W} right) ~~~~ "if" abs{ overline m^s - b_{r,W} - N_W^s } < 0.25'),
('p', 'The wide lanes are then kept fixed, and $N_1$ is estimated with receiver code and phase clocks. When the reference satellite changes from $q$ to $p$, the ambiguities of that system are shifted by an integer, $N_j leftarrow N_j - N_p$, with the linear transformation ${hat bold x} leftarrow bold T {hat bold x}$, $bold P leftarrow bold T bold P bold T^T$. Converged and fixed arcs are thus kept, instead of restarting all ambiguities of the system.'),
('p', '**Product check.** The analysis centre of each product is read from its file header: the agency of the SP3 file, the “ANALYSIS CENTER” record of the clock file, the agency of the Bias-SINEX file and the creator of the ORBEX file. A phase-bias file from another centre than the clock file is rejected, because its biases do not make the ambiguities integer with these clocks.'),

('h2', '2.5. Integer decision'),

('p', 'The wide lanes are fixed first, then $N_1$ conditioned on the fixed wide lanes. Both stages follow the same steps.'),
('p', '**Candidate selection and order.** A candidate needs an arc of at least 15 min and the minimum number of epochs, an elevation of at least 15° and a reference satellite covered by the product. The fraction and standard deviation gates of PRIDE PPP-AR (Geng et al., 2019b), a fractional part below 0.20 cycle (wide lane) or 0.15 cycle ($N_1$) and a standard deviation below 0.25 cycle, are optional. They suit a batch solution, in which every ambiguity has already converged, but in a sequential filter they reject arcs that are still converging and delay the first fix (Section 3.6), so they are off by default. The candidates are ordered by the probability of rounding to the nearest integer (Dong and Bock, 1989; Ge et al., 2005),'),
('eq', 'P_0 = 1 - sum from{n=1} to{infinity} left( func erfc left( {n - b} over {sqrt{2} %sigma} right) - func erfc left( {n + b} over {sqrt{2} %sigma} right) right), ~~~~ b = abs{ hat a - "round"(hat a) }'),
('p', '**Integer least squares and partial fixing.** The ILS problem'),
('eq', '{check bold a} = "arg" min from{bold a in setZ^n} ( {hat bold a} - bold a )^T bold Q_{hat a}^{-1} ( {hat bold a} - bold a )'),
('p', 'is solved with LAMBDA (Teunissen, 1995; de Jonge et al., 1996) after the decorrelating integer transformation $bold z = bold Z^T bold a$, with $bold Q_{hat z} = bold Z^T bold Q_{hat a} bold Z = bold L^T bold D bold L$. The bootstrapped success rate of a subset of decorrelated ambiguities is computed from their conditional variances $%sigma_{i|I}^2$, the diagonal of $bold D$ (Verhagen et al., 2013):'),
('eq', 'P_{"IB"} = prod from{i} func erf left( 1 over {2 sqrt 2 %sigma_{i|I}} right)'),
('p', 'and the largest subset of the last decorrelated ambiguities with $P_{"IB"} >= P_{"min"}$ = 0.999 is fixed (Verhagen et al., 2011; Psychas et al., 2020).'),
('p', '**Acceptance with the FFRT.** Following Verhagen et al. (2011), let $q_1 <= q_2$ be the squared norms of the best and second-best integer candidates and $%mu = q_2 / q_1$. The solution is accepted if $P_{"IB"} >= 1 - P_f$. Otherwise it is accepted if $%mu >= %mu(P_f, bold Q_{hat z})$, where the threshold is obtained at every epoch from $N_{"MC"} = 3000$ simulated float vectors ${hat bold z}_k sim N(0, bold Q_{hat z})$:'),
('eq', '%mu(P_f, bold Q_{hat z}) = "min" lbrace %mu^{"*"} : 1 over N_{"MC"} sum from{k=1} to{N_{"MC"}} "I" left( {check bold z}_k <> 0 and %mu_k >= %mu^{"*"} right) <= P_f rbrace'),
('p', 'with $P_f = 0.001$. In this simulation the integers of each sample are obtained by bootstrapping the decorrelated ambiguities, and the second candidate is the best unit neighbour, whose squared norm is $q_1 + {(bold Q_{hat z}^{-1})}_{ii} - 2 abs{ {( bold Q_{hat z}^{-1} ( {hat bold z}_k - {check bold z}_k ) )}_i }$. **Satellite exclusion.** If the test fails, the candidate with the lowest $P_0$ is removed and the stage is repeated, at most twice and as long as at least four ambiguities remain. **Fixed solution.** With the integer combinations $bold G {hat bold a}$ set to ${check bold z}$, the state and covariance are'),
('eq', '{check bold x} = {hat bold x} + bold P bold G^T (bold G bold P bold G^T)^{-1} ( {check bold z} - bold G {hat bold x} ), ~~~~ {check bold P} = bold P - bold P bold G^T (bold G bold P bold G^T)^{-1} bold G bold P'),
('p', 'The fixed solution is kept only if its normalised residual $bold v^T bold R^{-1} bold v / m$ is at most 9. This is a check of the functional model, not a test of the integers. Fixed values do not enter the float filter unless fix-and-hold is selected. In that case, integers that remain the same for ten epochs are added as pseudo-observations $a_j = {check a}_j$ with a standard deviation of 0.001 cycle.'),

('h2', '2.6. Machine-learning components'),

('p', '**Learned stochastic model.** A gradient-boosted regression $hat g$ is fitted to the logarithm of the squared post-fit residuals of a first float solution. The features $bold f$ are $sin e$, the signal-to-noise ratios and the logarithm of the arc length. Because the logarithm of a $%chi_1^2$ variable has a negative mean, the variance is'),
('eq', '{hat %sigma}^2(bold f) = exp left( hat g(bold f) - E left[ ln %chi_1^2 right] right), ~~~~ E left[ ln %chi_1^2 right] approx -1.2704'),
('p', '**Acceptance test trained with biases.** For each integer stage, a classifier estimates the probability that the fixed integers are correct from'),
('eq', 'bold f_{"AR"} = left( bold f_{"formal"}^T, bold f_{"bias"}^T right)^T'),
('eq', 'bold f_{"formal"} = left( ln %mu, "logit" P_{"IB"}, "logit" P_{"ILS"}^{"up"}, ln " ADOP", n, n / n_{"float"}, ln(1 + q_1/n), ln(1 + q_2/n), s_{"N1"} right)'),
('eq', 'bold f_{"bias"} = left( ln {%mu} over {%mu(P_f)}, "logit" "min" P_0, "max" abs{ {hat bold a} - "round"({hat bold a}) } right)'),
('p', 'where $P_{"ILS"}^{"up"}$ is the ADOP-based upper bound of the ILS success rate, $n$ the size of the fixed subset and $s_{"N1"}$ the stage. The formal features do not change when the float ambiguities are biased; the three bias features (the margin to the FFRT threshold, the smallest $P_0$ and the largest fractional part) do. The classifier is trained on simulated float solutions of the PPP-AR model in which the covariance is mis-scaled by $%kappa sim exp N(0, 0.4^2)$ and, in 20% of the samples, one or two ambiguities are shifted by up to 0.25 cycle. Its probabilities are calibrated with isotonic regression on a separate sample, so that a threshold of 0.99 means an estimated probability of 99% that the integers are correct.'),
('p', '**Fixability model trained on the same session.** A first run of the AR filter without fixing gives the float ambiguities at every epoch. If an arc ends with $%sigma < 0.05$ cycle and a fractional part below 0.10 cycle, its integer $a^{"*"}$ is known afterwards. The $N_1$ ambiguity is conditioned on this wide lane:'),
('eq:cond', '{tilde N}_1 = {hat N}_1 + {%sigma_{N_1 N_W}} over {%sigma_{N_W}^2} ( N_W^{"*"} - {hat N}_W ), ~~~~ {tilde %sigma}_{N_1}^2 = %sigma_{N_1}^2 - {%sigma_{N_1 N_W}^2} over {%sigma_{N_W}^2}'),
('p', 'Each earlier epoch $t$ of the arc gets the label $y_t = "I"( "round"(hat a_t) = a^{"*"} )$, and an arc that converges to a non-integer value is labelled as not fixable. A gradient-boosted model then estimates $P(y = 1 | bold f_{"fix"})$ from quantities known at epoch $t$, $bold f_{"fix"} = (ln %sigma, abs{ hat a - "round" hat a }, P_0, sin e, ln(1 + n_{"arc"}), s_{"N1"})$. The model is cross-fitted by satellite, so that no satellite is scored by a model trained on its own labels. In the second run this probability replaces $P_0$ in the ordering, candidates below 0.5 are dropped, and the integer search starts only when at least four candidates exceed this value.'),
('p', '**Process noise of the FCB filter.** In the EKF+ML FCB estimator (Section 2.7), the random-walk variance of each satellite FCB is estimated from the block-to-block changes $%DELTA s_k$ of a first filter run, using the median absolute deviation and subtracting the formal variance:'),
('eq', 'q^s = 1 over {%DELTA t} "min" left( "max" left( (1.4826 " MAD"(%DELTA s))^2 - " median"( %sigma_k^2 + %sigma_{k-1}^2 ), q_{"min"} right), q_{"max"} right)'),
('p', 'with $sqrt{q_{"min"}} = 0.002$ and $sqrt{q_{"max"}} = 0.2$ cycle per block.'),

('h2', '2.7. Network FCB estimation'),

('p', 'Each network station is processed with the float EKF, using the same products and models as the user. A second run holds the station coordinate fixed at the mean of the last hour of the first run. The float ambiguity of station $i$ and satellite $j$ in cycles is'),
('eq', 'b_{ij} = n_{ij} + r_i + s_j + %varepsilon_{ij}'),
('p', 'with the integer $n_{ij}$ and the receiver and satellite fractional biases $r_i$ and $s_j$; the bias of one receiver is set to zero. Wide-lane values are arc means of the MW combination (Ge et al., 2008). After the wide lanes are fixed, the narrow-lane value at the end of each 15 min block is'),
('eq', 'b_{ij}^{"NL"} = { B_{"IF",ij} - %beta N_{W,ij} } over %lambda_N'),
('p', 'Three estimators are implemented. The batch estimator (Li and Zhang, 2012; Li et al., 2016) solves $r_i$ and $s_j$ in each block by weighted least squares and fixes the ambiguities whose fractional residual is below 0.25 cycle, repeating until no new ambiguity is fixed. The EKF estimator treats $r_i$ and $s_j$ as random walks and fixes the integers against the predicted state, so information is carried from one block to the next. The EKF+ML estimator adds the learned process noise of Section 2.6 and weights each ambiguity with a gradient-boosted estimate of its residual variance. A satellite FCB of a block is kept only if at least $n_c = "min"(10, "max"(1, lfloor N_{"st"} / 10 rfloor))$ of the $N_{"st"}$ stations contribute to it. The FCBs are written for L1/L2 (E1/E5a) as'),
('eq', 'b_1^s = s_{"NL"}^s - %beta over %lambda_N s_{"WL"}^s, ~~~~ b_2^s = b_1^s - s_{"WL"}^s'),
('p', 'Each estimator is tested by leaving one station out: the FCBs are estimated without station $k$ and applied to the ambiguities of $k$. The receiver part is removed by a circular mean in each block, and the remaining fractions $%epsilon$ give'),
('eq', '" RMS"_k = sqrt{ 1 over n_k sum %epsilon^2 }, ~~~~ p_{0.15} = 1 over n_k sum "I"( abs %epsilon < 0.15 )'),
('p', 'For networks with more than 20 stations, 20 evenly spread stations are left out in turn. The stations are processed in parallel.'),

('h2', '2.8. Narrow-lane product test'),

('p', 'The test holds the receiver coordinates at a known value, either a reference coordinate or the mean of the last hour of a converged float solution, runs the AR filter without fixing, and every 15 min collects the fractions ${tilde N}_1 - "round"({tilde N}_1)$ (Eq. {eq:cond}) of arcs that are longer than 30 min, have ${tilde %sigma}_{N_1} < 0.05$ cycle and a wide-lane fraction below 0.15 cycle. For uniformly distributed fractions, 20% lie within ±0.10 cycle and 30% within ±0.15 cycle; with consistent products most fractions lie within ±0.10 cycle (Ge et al., 2008; Dai et al., 2019). The circular mean of each satellite shows errors that affect single satellites, and running the test with and without the models of Section 2.2 separates product errors from model errors.'),
]

